Good evening, everyone. Allow me to introduce Officer Cameron Perlitt and Mr. Michael Fowler. We're here to recognize Officer Perlitt and Officer Joel Stevens, who couldn't be here tonight, for their actions that saved Mr. Fowler's life.

31
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:24,000
On August 8, Officer Stevens and Perlitt were responding to what appeared to be a traffic accident at Dr. Joseph E. Lowry Boulevard in St. Clair. When Officer Perlitt approached Mr. Fowler's vehicle, he found him unconscious and in serious medical distress.

32
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The officers could not get the door open, so Officer Stevens had to break the passenger window out in order to get access to Mr. Fowler. Officer Perlitt got inside and found that Mr. Fowler was barely breathing. Both officers worked to get him out of the vehicle, and then Officer Stevens assessed Mr. Fowler and determined he was no longer breathing and did not have a pulse.

33
00:05:47,000 --> 00:06:13,000
So Officer Perlitt immediately began CPR while Officer Stevens supported Mr. Fowler's head and neck and assisted until a harmful fire and rescue could arrive. This all happened in just a matter of minutes. But in those minutes, these officers recognized what was happening, acted quickly, and put their training to work when somebody was in critical need. And this evening, we have the privilege of seeing this person that they were fighting to save.

34
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Mr. Fowler, we're so glad that you're here. We're grateful that our officers were able to be of assistance to you when you needed it. Officer Perlitt, your quick response, your training, your ability to remain calm in a critical situation certainly made a difference.

35
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:43,000
This is exactly what we expect from our officers when the moment calls for it, to recognize what needs to be done and to do everything possible to protect and preserve lives.

36
